	(text_box "\n=== NET LABEL NAMING CONVENTION ===\nA/B suffix (ex. GPIO_14A, GPIO_14B):\nTwo pins that are physically related on the FPGA package (adjacent\npair number) but electrically INDEPENDENT, meaning each carries its own\nunrelated signal. NOT a differential pair.\n\n+/- suffix (ex. GPIO_8+, GPIO_8-):\nA differential pair - the two pins are deliberately driven\nas opposites of each other to carry ONE signal.\n\nWhether a given physical pin pair is used as A/B or +/- is a design\nchoice made on the sheet where it's wired - not a fixed property of\nthe silicon. Check the destination, not just the pin name, if unsure.\n\nSwapping + and - on a pair is allowed only if the far end is updated\nto match (or the polarity is inverted in the FPGA logic).\n\n\n=== VOLTAGE MAP ===\nVOLTAGE MAP (VCCIO per bank):\nBank 0   3.3V              single-ended only - GPIO expansion header\nBank 1   3.3V              GPDI video output (emulated differential) + GPIO exp.\nBank 2   3.3V              SDRAM\nBank 3   3.3V              SDRAM\nBank 6   3.3V              USB-UART (FT231XS), SD card\nBank 7   2.5V / 3.3V       GPIO expansion header (+/- pairs), audio/video, LEDs\n                           VCCIO7 = +2V5_3V3 (selectable rail). Verify the\n                           selected voltage before connecting external signals.\nBank 8   3.3V              Dedicated config/JTAG pins (not general GPIO)\n\nOther rails: VCC (core) 1.1V, VCCAUX 2.5V.\nSignals may move between banks only if the banks share the same VCCIO.\nBank 7 signals at 2.5V are NOT 3.3V compatible.\n\n\n=== PIN ASSIGNMENT METHODOLOGY ===\nPIN ASSIGNMENT METHODOLOGY:\nSource: Lattice ECP5 caBGA381 pin migration data (per-device column), this\nspreadsheet confirms bank, dual function, and differential status for any\nphysical ball.\n\nRefer to bank/voltage map first - confirms what's already committed before\nhunting for a free pin.\n\nBefore assigning a pin, check:\n  - bank: must match the voltage already committed to that bank\n  - dual function: some balls double as clock inputs - don't collide\n  - differential: only matters if building an actual diff pair (see\n    A/B vs +/- note)\n\nBefore wiring any IC's pins, verify against its own datasheet, never assume\na reference design's values/pins carry over to a different part or voltage combo.\n\nUNUSED GPIO PINS:\nUnused ordinary GPIOs are left as No Connect - no external pull resistor\nrequired. The ECP5 has internal weak pulls enabled by default on every I/O\n(tri-stated with weak pull-down at power-up). Only add an external pull if\nthe internal one is intentionally disabled in the pin constraints file.\nSource: Lattice Answer Database #738.\n\nGPDI DIFFERENTIAL SIGNALING:\nGPDI does NOT use the ECP5's dedicated True-LVDS hardware driver. It uses\n\"emulated\" differential signaling - ordinary LVCMOS pins driven as a\ncomplementary pair, using external resistors/caps to approximate the signal\nlevels. This works on any confirmed A/B pin pair, True-LVDS-flagged or not,\nand needs no special 2.5V bank. Source: Lattice ECP5 sysI/O Usage Guide,\nFPGA-TN-02032.\n\nTrue LVDS output on the GPIO +/- pairs would require bank 7 at 2.5V AND a\npin pair that supports it. Confirm in the sysI/O Usage Guide before use.\n\n=== PCB DESIGN RULES (NEW IN v1.1) ===\nPCB DESIGN RULES:\nStackup (4 layers): F.Cu signal / In1 GND / In2 PWR / B.Cu signal.\n\nControlled impedance:\n  - USB (including FT231XS D+/D-):  90 ohm differential\n  - GPDI / HDMI pairs:              100 ohm differential, length matched in-pair\n  - GPIO +/- pairs:                 same spec as GPDI\n  - SDRAM:                          50 ohm single-ended target (not critical at\n                                    100 MHz), length matched\n  - Camera (OV7670, parallel DVP), SD card, ADC, audio, LEDs, UART:\n                                    no impedance control, default net class\nTrace widths and gaps: see Net Classes in the PCB Board Setup.\n\nLayer rules:\n  - High-speed nets (SDRAM, GPDI, USB) stay on F.Cu / B.Cu.\n  - Do not cut In1 (GND) under high-speed traces.\n  - Low-speed GPIO may be routed on In2 only if short, away from the BGA core\n    pour, and not across a rail split.\n  - ESD / TVS diodes sit at the connector with no stub.\n  - Power traces are sized by current, pours for the 1.1V core.\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
